{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Python 2 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Python 2 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nIn one very old text file there was written Great Wisdom. This Wisdom was so Great that nobody could decipher it, even Phong \u2014 the oldest among the inhabitants of Mainframe. But still he managed to get some information from there. For example, he managed to learn that User launches games for pleasure \u2014 and then terrible Game Cubes fall down on the city, bringing death to those modules, who cannot win the game...For sure, as guard Bob appeared in Mainframe many modules stopped fearing Game Cubes. Because Bob (as he is alive yet) has never been defeated by User, and he always meddles with Game Cubes, because he is programmed to this.However, unpleasant situations can happen, when a Game Cube falls down on Lost Angles. Because there lives a nasty virus \u2014 Hexadecimal, who is... mmm... very strange. And she likes to play very much. So, willy-nilly, Bob has to play with her first, and then with User.This time Hexadecimal invented the following entertainment: Bob has to leap over binary search trees with n nodes. We should remind you that a binary search tree is a binary tree, each node has a distinct key, for each node the following is true: the left sub-tree of a node contains only nodes with keys less than the node's key, the right sub-tree of a node contains only nodes with keys greater than the node's key. All the keys are different positive integer numbers from 1 to n. Each node of such a tree can have up to two children, or have no children at all (in the case when a node is a leaf).In Hexadecimal's game all the trees are different, but the height of each is not lower than h. In this problem \u00abheight\u00bb stands for the maximum amount of nodes on the way from the root to the remotest leaf, the root node and the leaf itself included. When Bob leaps over a tree, it disappears. Bob gets the access to a Cube, when there are no trees left. He knows how many trees he will have to leap over in the worst case. And you?\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Python 2 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nfrom math import *\nfrom Queue import *\nfrom sys import *\nfrom datetime import *\n\nnMax, hMax = map(int, raw_input().split())\ntable = [[0] * (nMax + 1) for _ in range(nMax + 1)]\ntable[0][0] = 1\n\nfor i in range(1, nMax + 1):\n    table[i][i] = 2 ** (i - 1)\n\nfor h in range(1, nMax + 1):\n    for n in range(h + 1, nMax + 1):\n        total = 0\n        for i in range(1, n + 1):\n            for j in range(h):\n                total += table[i - 1][h - 1] * table[n - i][j]\n            for j in range(h):\n                total += table[i - 1][j] * table[n - i][h - 1]\n            total -= table[i - 1][h - 1] * table[n - i][h - 1]\n        table[n][h] = total\n\nres = 0\nfor h in range(hMax, nMax + 1):\n    res += table[nMax][h]\nprint res\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nfrom math import *\nfrom Queue import *\nfrom sys import *\nfrom datetime import *\n\nnMax, hMax = map(int, raw_input().split())\n\n# dp[n][h] = number of binary search trees with n nodes and height exactly h\ndp = [[0] * (nMax + 1) for _ in range(nMax + 1)]\n\n# Base case: empty tree has height 0\ndp[0][0] = 1\n\n# For a tree with n nodes and height h, we consider each node i (1 <= i <= n) as root\n# Left subtree has i-1 nodes, right subtree has n-i nodes\n# The height of the tree is 1 + max(height of left subtree, height of right subtree)\n\n# First, compute dp for all n and h\nfor n in range(1, nMax + 1):\n    for h in range(1, n + 1):  # minimum height is 1 (for n=1), max height is n (skewed tree)\n        total = 0\n        for root in range(1, n + 1):\n            left_nodes = root - 1\n            right_nodes = n - root\n            \n            # For the tree to have height h, one of the subtrees must have height h-1\n            # and the other can have height at most h-1\n            # So we count: (left height = h-1 and right height <= h-1) + (right height = h-1 and left height <= h-1) - (both height = h-1)\n            \n            left_sum = 0\n            right_sum = 0\n            \n            # Sum of all trees with left_nodes nodes and height <= h-2\n            for lh in range(0, h - 1):\n                left_sum += dp[left_nodes][lh] if left_nodes >= 0 else 0\n            \n            # Sum of all trees with right_nodes nodes and height <= h-2\n            for rh in range(0, h - 1):\n                right_sum += dp[right_nodes][rh] if right_nodes >= 0 else 0\n            \n            # Case 1: left subtree has height exactly h-1, right subtree has height <= h-1\n            if left_nodes >= 0:\n                total += dp[left_nodes][h - 1] * (dp[right_nodes][h - 1] + right_sum)\n            \n            # Case 2: right subtree has height exactly h-1, left subtree has height < h-1 (to avoid double counting)\n            if right_nodes >= 0:\n                total += (dp[left_nodes][h - 1] + left_sum) * dp[right_nodes][h - 1]\n            \n            # Wait, this double counts the case where both subtrees have height h-1\n            # Let's recalculate more carefully:\n            total = 0\n            for lh in range(0, h):\n                for rh in range(0, h):\n                    if max(lh, rh) == h - 1:  # one of them must be exactly h-1\n                        if left_nodes >= 0 and right_nodes >= 0:\n                            total += dp[left_nodes][lh] * dp[right_nodes][rh]\n        \n        dp[n][h] = total\n\n# Sum all trees with height >= hMax\nresult = 0\nfor h in range(hMax, nMax + 1):\n    result += dp[nMax][h]\n\nprint result\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.3\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [0.0, -8.892617915989831e-05, -1.311301275563892e-06, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -0.5984922051429749, -0.4762747585773468, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -0.16027498245239258, -1.549708758830093e-05, -0.25275108218193054, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -2.9205850296420977e-05, -3.933898824470816e-06, -4.172316494077677e-06, 0.0, -0.00016485285595990717, -1.0728830375228426e-06, -2.90866428258596e-05, -1.0728830375228426e-06, -0.00020311199477873743, -0.08439335227012634, -0.016072941944003105, 0.0, 0.0, -0.0001691436773398891, -4.768370445162873e-07, -1.1086402082582936e-05, -1.5497195136049413e-06, 0.0, -1.1086402082582936e-05, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -8.344646857949556e-07, -3.099436753473128e-06, -0.06198430806398392, -0.000878544058650732, -0.1412379890680313, -0.0021615491714328527, -6.90197994117625e-05, -2.9802276912960224e-06, -3.576272320060525e-06, -0.00010787858627736568, -6.079655122448457e-06, -8.344646857949556e-07, -0.3175373375415802, -4.255681051290594e-05, -6.794906312279636e-06, -3.099436753473128e-06, -4.768370445162873e-07, -0.001347587094642222, -0.008682476356625557, -0.0016191004542633891, -0.0009411911014467478, -5.9960475482512265e-05, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -0.06998558342456818, -7.152555099310121e-07, -0.00016497205069754273, -1.4305104514278355e-06, -0.00020597243565134704, -1.4305104